meone is cold and they see the lion, they need the tiger.\n\n2. **Direct Evidence:**.\n   - The mouse sees the lion. (From the context, the mouse sees the lion.).\n   - If someone sees the lion, they are cold. (From the context, if someone sees the lion, they are cold.).\n   - Therefore, the mouse, who sees the lion, is cold.\n\n3. **Indirect Evidence:**.\n   - The tiger needs the lion and the tiger needs the mouse. (From the context, the tiger needs the lion and the tiger needs the mouse.).\n   - If someone needs the tiger and they need the mouse, they are cold. (From the context, if someone needs the tiger and they need the mouse, they are cold.).\n   - Since the tiger needs the lion and the tiger needs the mouse, and the tiger is green (all round people are green), the tiger is a round green person.\n   - If someone needs the tiger, the tiger sees the lion. (From the context, if someone needs the tiger, the tiger sees the lion.).\n   - Since the tiger needs the lion and the tiger sees the lion, the tiger needs the lion.\n   - Therefore, the tiger needs the lion, and the tiger needs the mouse, making the tiger cold.\n   - Since the tiger is cold and the tiger sees the lion, the tiger needs the lion.\n   - Since the tiger needs the lion, the lion is cold.\n   - Since the lion eats the mouse, and the lion is cold, the mouse is cold.\n\n4. **Conclusion:**.\n   - The mouse is cold based on the given information. Therefore, my final option is: ']]
