ot directly relevant to the lion\'s roundness.\n\n6. **If something is nice and green then it visits the bald eagle.**.\n   - This statement is not directly relevant to the lion\'s roundness.\n\n7. **The cat is nice and green.**.\n   - Since the cat is nice and green, it must visit the bald eagle (from the previous statement).\n\n8. **The cat likes the bald eagle.**.\n   - This is given directly.\n\n9. **The cat likes the dog.**.\n   - This is given directly.\n\n10. **The cat visits the dog.**.\n    - This is given directly.\n\n11. **The dog eats the cat.**.\n    - This is given directly.\n\n12. **The dog is big.**.\n    - This is given directly.\n\n13. **The dog is blue.**.\n    - This is given directly.\n\n14. **The lion is blue.**.\n    - This is given directly.\n\n15. **If something eats the cat and the cat eats the dog then the cat eats the lion.**.\n    - This statement is not directly relevant to the lion\'s roundness.\n\n16. **If the dog eats the cat and the cat eats the dog then the cat eats the lion.**.\n    - This statement is not directly relevant to the lion\'s roundness.\n\n17. **If something visits the dog and it eats the lion then the lion is round.**.\n    - This statement is not directly relevant to the lion\'s roundness.\n\nFrom the given information, we can see that:.\n- The lion is blue.\n- The lion is not mentioned to be round or big.\n- There is no direct information about the lion\'s roundness.\n\nGiven that the lion is blue and there is no information that directly states or implies the lion\'s roundness, we cannot definitively conclude whether the lion is round or not. Therefore, my final option is: ']]
